"""Foundation editorial guides; these are not reports of completed activities."""
FOUNDATION_BLOGS = [
    ('choose-your-gecp-pathway', 'Find your starting point in the GECP programme family', 'GECP pathways', [
        ('Start with the question you are trying to answer', 'The GECP programme family brings together several ways to connect education with practical opportunity. You do not need to understand the whole network before making an enquiry. Start by describing what you want to learn, the decision you face or the experience you want to contribute. That purpose helps identify a relevant first conversation.'),
        ('Connect your interests with a programme', 'GCCS focuses on complementary education and lifelong learning, while PEC explores education and career pathways. WWI connects learning with the world of work. RENGTECH and TRADESTART address practical technical learning and enterprise preparation. SPIN connects skills with collaboration, and GSEVS creates pathways for experienced professionals to share expertise.'),
        ('Look for the connections between programmes', 'Educational resources, communication and knowledge organisation also shape a learning journey. ERAid focuses on resource access; EVM helps knowledge reach wider audiences; DRICD supports research and information curation; and TKRD explores the preservation of experience-based knowledge. These are connected areas of focus, rather than a promise that every proposed service is currently available.'),
        ('Prepare a useful first enquiry', 'Choose the programme closest to your immediate interest and use its enquiry form. Explain your starting point, intended outcome and relevant availability. Keep the reference provided after submission. Current participation requirements, schedules and delivery arrangements are confirmed by the responsible team.'),
    ]),
    ('learning-experience-community', 'Why learning, experience and community belong in the same conversation', 'Lifelong learning', [
        ('Learning happens in different settings', 'Formal study is one part of a learning journey. People also develop capabilities through practice, work, observation and conversation. Recognising these settings makes it easier to describe what someone already knows and where further support would help. It also creates space for people whose experience does not fit neatly into a single qualification.'),
        ('Experience becomes useful when it can be shared', 'An experienced practitioner can explain how a decision was made, what signals mattered and what changed after feedback. A learner can bring a fresh question or a different context. The exchange becomes more useful when both participants listen and make the reasoning visible, rather than treating experience as a fixed answer.'),
        ('Communities provide context for practice', 'A practical challenge gives learning a purpose. It can help a group identify the skills, resources and collaboration needed to take a next step. The intended beneficiaries should be part of understanding that challenge. A clear purpose helps keep a learning activity connected to the people it is meant to serve.'),
        ('Turn a conversation into a next step', 'GECP’s connected programme approach makes room for learning, mentoring, workplace exposure and knowledge preservation. A useful starting point is modest: one question, one contribution or one capability to develop. Discuss that starting point with the relevant programme team before assuming a particular activity or opportunity is available.'),
    ]),
    ('partnership-starting-questions', 'A practical starting point for a GECP partnership conversation', 'Partnerships', [
        ('Describe the shared purpose', 'A partnership enquiry should explain the learning or community need you want to address. Introduce your organisation and the people you serve, then describe the change you hope a collaboration could support. A clear purpose gives both organisations a basis for discussing whether their interests and capabilities align.'),
        ('Identify what each partner can contribute', 'Contributions may include practitioner knowledge, learning spaces, resources, mentoring or meaningful assignments. Be specific about what you can offer and what support you need. Avoid treating an initial expression of interest as an agreed commitment; it is the beginning of a discussion about suitability and capacity.'),
        ('Consider how the work would be supported', 'Discuss the people responsible, the intended participants, communication and the information needed to plan responsibly. A useful contribution also needs a way to be reviewed. Define what you would want to learn from an initial activity and how that learning could inform a later decision.'),
        ('Use the enquiry to open a conversation', 'The GECP partnership form asks for your organisation, role and proposed collaboration. Share enough context for the team to route the enquiry without including confidential documents. Programme scope, responsibilities and any formal terms are discussed separately; submitting the form does not create a partnership agreement.'),
    ]),
]
